# Keep the build context to what the image actually needs.
#
# Before this existed, `COPY backend/ ./` was dragging in backend/.venv (4,538
# files, 101 MB) and __pycache__ on every build: a 173 MB context for an image
# whose real content is a few megabytes.
#
# The `**/` prefixes are load-bearing. A bare `.venv` matches only `./.venv`; the
# virtualenv here is at `backend/.venv`, so without `**/` it is silently copied
# in and the image ships a 100 MB directory of Windows-built wheels that nothing
# can import. A `.dockerignore` that looks right and matches nothing is worse
# than none, because it reads as if the problem is handled.

**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/*.egg-info/

**/node_modules/
frontend/dist/

.git/
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
**/*.log

# Local scratch and verification artifacts.
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# NOTE on the database.
#
# `backend/tabiko.db` is deliberately NOT excluded, so a local build produces a
# container that actually contains the city.
#
# That is a temporary convenience and it has a real caveat: the file is
# gitignored, so a CI-built or clone-built image has no database and serves
# `{"places": 0}`. Nothing fails, health checks pass, and the app looks healthy
# while being empty. CI cannot catch it, because CI checks out from git and so
# has no data either.
#
# The fix is to make the city reproducible from source -- an importer that
# rebuilds the database from a pinned bbox and date -- at which point this file
# should exclude the database too, like every other build artifact. Until then,
# keep this exclusion off, or your local container goes blank.
